from lib import *

section('bio_electives', 'Electives and frontiers',
 'Specialised topics that build on the core: marine, cancer, drugs, disease spread, synthetic biology and life beyond Earth.',
 'Electives let you go deeper in an area you like: oceans, cancer, medicine, public health, engineering life or searching for it elsewhere.',
 'Each elective applies core ideas (evolution, cell biology, ecology, statistics) to a specialised problem, often with open scientific questions.',
 'Frontier topics have partly settled and partly unsettled science, so uncertainty is flagged. Skills such as modelling, computing and ethics are as important as facts here.',
 'cancer_biology pharmacology')
n('bio_marine_biology','Marine biology','concept','bio_biomes bio_osmoregulation','Life in the ocean.',
 'The ocean covers ≈ 71% of Earth and contains most of its living space. Plankton make about half of the world\'s oxygen-producing photosynthesis.',
 'Zones: intertidal, pelagic, benthic and deep sea. Coral reefs, kelp forests and hydrothermal vents host distinct communities.',
 'Marine food webs are driven by phytoplankton (turnover days) and mesopelagic fish. Chemosynthetic symbioses at vents (tubeworms) were discovered in 1977. Overfishing, warming and acidification are key threats; ≈ 35% of fish stocks are overfished (FAO).',
 'Ocean share of Earth|≈ 71%;Overfished stocks (FAO)|≈ 35%', 'BIO:44.4 BIO:23.4', k='oceans_circulation ocean_acidification', w='place:pond')
n('bio_cancer_hallmarks','Cancer biology and hallmarks','concept','bio_cancer_gene_regulation bio_apoptosis','How cells escape normal control.',
 'Cancer is uncontrolled cell growth caused by accumulated gene changes. Tumours can invade tissues and spread (metastasis).',
 'Hallmarks include sustained proliferation, evading suppressors, resisting death, immortality, angiogenesis, invasion, altered metabolism and immune evasion.',
 'Clonal evolution within a tumour drives drug resistance. Targeted therapies (imatinib for BCR-ABL, trastuzumab for HER2) and immunotherapy have improved outcomes. About 1 in 5 people develop cancer in a lifetime; ≈ 10 million deaths yearly worldwide.',
 'Global cancer deaths|≈ 10 million/yr;Imatinib approval|2001', 'BIO:10.4 BIO:16.7', k='cancer_biology', w='place:cell')
n('bio_pharmacology_adme','Pharmacology: drug action and ADME','concept','bio_enzyme_inhibition bio_gpcr_signaling','How drugs work and move in the body.',
 'Drugs bind targets (receptors, enzymes, channels) and change their activity. The body absorbs, distributes, metabolises and excretes them (ADME).',
 'Dose–response curves give potency (EC₅₀) and efficacy. The liver\'s cytochrome P450 enzymes metabolise most drugs.',
 'Half-life t½ = 0.693·Vd/CL determines dosing intervals. Drug–drug interactions via CYP3A4 are clinically important. Pharmacogenomics (e.g. CYP2C19 and clopidogrel) personalises therapy.',
 'Steady state reached after|≈ 5 half-lives;Drug-metabolising enzyme family|CYP450', 'ANP:24.5', f='t½ = 0.693·Vd/CL', k='pharmacology', w='mol:aspirin', mol='aspirin paracetamol ibuprofen morphine caffeine metformin')
n('bio_toxicology','Toxicology and dose–response','concept','bio_pharmacology_adme','The dose makes the poison.',
 'Substances are harmful above some dose. LD₅₀ is the dose that kills half of test animals; NOAEL is the highest dose with no observed harm.',
 'Paracelsus: "the dose makes the poison". Heavy metals, pesticides and drugs have different mechanisms and thresholds.',
 'Some toxicants show hormesis (low-dose benefit) or non-monotonic dose responses. Regulators use safety factors (often 100×) from NOAEL to set reference doses. Bioaccumulation and biomagnification (DDT, mercury) raise exposure up the food web.',
 'Typical safety factor|≈ 100×;DDT in top predators|biomagnified ≈ 10⁶×', 'CHE:21.6', k='pharmacology', w='mol:cisplatin', mol='cisplatin nicotine hydrogen_cyanide carbon_monoxide')
n('bio_epidemiology','Epidemiology and study design','concept','bio_pathogenesis bio_hypothesis_testing','Studying disease in populations.',
 'Epidemiologists measure how often disease occurs and what raises or lowers risk. Key measures are incidence, prevalence and relative risk.',
 'Designs: cohort, case–control, cross-sectional and randomised trials. John Snow\'s 1854 Broad Street map linked cholera to a water pump.',
 'Confounding, selection bias and measurement error threaten causal inference; Bradford Hill criteria and causal diagrams help. Relative risk RR = [a/(a+b)]/[c/(c+d)], odds ratio OR = ad/bc.',
 'Snow\'s cholera study|1854;Relative risk|ratio of risks', 'MIC:16.1 MIC:16.2 MIC:16.3 MIC:16.4', f='OR = (a·d)/(b·c)', k='medicine_evidence', w='')
n('bio_epidemic_modeling','Epidemic modelling (SIR)','technique','bio_epidemiology bio_ode_models_biology','Modelling outbreaks with equations.',
 'The SIR model splits a population into susceptible, infected and recovered groups and tracks flows between them.',
 'An epidemic grows when R₀ > 1. Vaccination, distancing and treatment aim to bring the effective reproduction number R below 1.',
 'dS/dt = −βSI/N, dI/dt = βSI/N − γI, dR/dt = γI; R₀ = β/γ. Final size solves ln(S∞/S₀) = R₀(S∞ − 1). Extensions: SEIR, age structure, networks and stochastic effects; parameter uncertainty is considerable.',
 'R₀|β/γ;Herd immunity threshold|1 − 1/R₀', 'MIC:16.1', f='dI/dt = βSI/N − γI;  R₀ = β/γ', k='networks_complexity', w='', lab='population:sir_epidemic')
n('bio_advanced_endocrinology','Endocrine disorders and hormone physiology','concept','bio_endocrine_comparative bio_metabolic_integration','When hormone systems go wrong.',
 'Diabetes, thyroid disease and Cushing\'s syndrome involve hormones out of balance. Many are treated by replacing or blocking hormones.',
 'Type 1 diabetes lacks insulin; type 2 has insulin resistance. Hypothyroidism slows metabolism; hyperthyroidism speeds it.',
 'Hormone axes (HPA, HPT, HPG) use negative feedback; set-point dysregulation shows in lab ratios such as TSH/T4. Endocrine disruptors (BPA) are studied for low-dose effects; evidence is mixed and debated.',
 'Diabetes prevalence (adults, global)|≈ 10–11%;Thyroid hormones|T3, T4', 'ANP:17.3 ANP:17.4 ANP:17.9', k='endocrine_physiology', w='thyroid', mol='thyroxine bisphenol_a cortisol')
n('bio_virology_advanced','Advanced virology: antivirals and evolution','concept','bio_retroviruses_hiv bio_emerging_viruses','Virus evolution and control.',
 'Antivirals block steps of the viral cycle: entry, copying or release. RNA viruses mutate fast, so drugs and vaccines may need updating.',
 'Flu drifts every season (antigenic drift) and occasionally shifts by swapping gene segments (shift). Combination therapy limits resistance in HIV and hepatitis C (cure rates > 95%).',
 'RNA virus mutation rates ≈ 10⁻⁶–10⁻⁴ per base per replication; error catastrophe limits viability. Deep mutational scanning maps escape. Viral metagenomics has revealed ≈ 10⁵ new viral species.',
 'RNA virus mutation rate|≈ 10⁻⁶–10⁻⁴ per base;HCV cure rate|> 95%', 'MIC:14.4 MIC:6.3 BIO:21.3', k='viruses', w='place:cell')
n('bio_bioinformatics_advanced','Advanced bioinformatics and machine learning','technique','bio_blast_databases bio_bayesian_inference_bio bio_transcriptomics','Learning from large biological data.',
 'Machine learning finds patterns in sequences, images and expression data. AlphaFold-type networks predict protein structure from sequence.',
 'Tools include clustering of single-cell data, variant-effect predictors and image segmentation of microscopy. Models need careful validation to avoid overfitting.',
 'Protein language models learn evolutionary constraints from sequence alone. Pitfalls: data leakage, batch effects, ancestry bias and interpretability. AlphaFold 2 was recognised with the 2024 Nobel Prize in Chemistry.',
 'AlphaFold Nobel|2024 (Hassabis, Jumper, Baker);Single-cell atlases|millions of cells', 'DSC:1.1', k='neural_nets', w='place:dna')
n('bio_conservation_genomics','Conservation genetics and genomics','technique','bio_inbreeding_conservation_genetics bio_conservation_strategies bio_ngs','Using DNA to save species.',
 'Genetic data show how diverse and how related individuals in small populations are, guiding breeding and relocation.',
 'Tools: microsatellites, SNP arrays, eDNA monitoring and genomic assessment of inbreeding. Genetic rescue helped Florida panthers.',
 'The 50/500 rule (now 100/1,000) is a rough guide to minimum Ne for short- and long-term viability. Genomic load can be measured directly. De-extinction proposals (woolly mammoth, thylacine) are scientifically and ethically contested.',
 'Ne rule of thumb|100 (short), 1,000 (long term);Thylacine extinction|1936', 'BIO:47.4', k='biodiversity_conservation', w='place:park')
n('bio_synthetic_biology','Synthetic biology','technique','bio_lac_operon bio_crispr_cas9','Engineering organisms with designed parts.',
 'Synthetic biologists design genetic circuits, pathways and even whole genomes using standard parts, and build them in cells.',
 'Examples: yeast making artemisinin precursor, engineered bacteria sensing molecules, and a minimal bacterial genome (JCVI-syn3.0, 473 genes). Biosecurity and ethics need attention.',
 'Design–build–test–learn cycles are accelerated by DNA synthesis and automation. Genetic toggle switch (2000) and repressilator (2000) were early circuits. Orthogonal ribosomes and expanded genetic alphabets (unnatural base pairs) extend the code.',
 'JCVI-syn3.0 genes|473;Repressilator|2000 (Elowitz, Leibler)', 'MIC:12.1 MIC:12.4', k='crispr_biotech evolution_algorithm', w='place:pond', lab='molecule:genetic_circuit')
n('bio_astrobiology','Astrobiology and the search for life','concept','bio_origin_of_life bio_microbial_metabolism','Life beyond Earth.',
 'Astrobiology asks where life might exist elsewhere. Targets include Mars, Europa and Enceladus and planets orbiting other stars.',
 'Habitable zones are where water could be liquid. Biosignatures include oxygen with methane, or organic patterns in rocks.',
 'No confirmed extraterrestrial life exists. Perseverance is collecting Mars samples; the 1996 Martian meteorite ALH84001 claim and the 2020 Venus phosphine claim remain unconfirmed or disputed. Extremophile limits (e.g., ≈ 122 °C, pH 0, high radiation) widen possible habitats.',
 'Confirmed alien life|none;Confirmed exoplanets|> 5,500', 'BIO:20.3', k='habitability exoplanets origin_of_life', c='C3',
 oq=['Whether life exists or ever existed beyond Earth is unknown.', 'How to reliably distinguish biosignatures from abiotic false positives is unresolved.'], w='place:earth')
n('bio_systems_biology_networks','Systems biology and biological networks','concept','bio_ode_models_biology bio_proteomics_omics','Cells as networks.',
 'Systems biology studies how many parts interact to produce cell behaviour. Networks of genes, proteins and metabolites are mapped and modelled.',
 'Network motifs (feedback, feed-forward loops) give circuits functions like switching, oscillation and noise filtering.',
 'Flux balance analysis predicts metabolic flows from stoichiometry. Gene-expression noise is both a nuisance and a feature (bet-hedging in bacteria). Scale-free claims for networks are controversial.',
 'E. coli metabolic model|≈ 2,700 reactions;Network motif example|feed-forward loop', 'BIO:17.5', k='networks_complexity', w='', lab='molecule:network_motifs')
n('bio_biophysics_elective','Biophysics of cells and molecules','concept','bio_thermo_laws bio_active_transport','Physics applied to living matter.',
 'Biophysics uses physical laws to explain life: diffusion in cells, forces from motor proteins, and electrical signals in nerves.',
 'Single-molecule techniques (optical tweezers, AFM) measure forces of ≈ 1–100 pN. Motor proteins like kinesin work near thermal-noise limits.',
 'At low Reynolds number (Re ≈ 10⁻⁵ for bacteria), inertia is negligible; flagella use non-reciprocal motion. Thermal energy kBT ≈ 4.1 pN·nm sets the scale of molecular events.',
 'kBT at 25 °C|≈ 4.1 pN·nm;Kinesin stall force|≈ 6 pN', 'UP1:1.1', f='kBT ≈ 4.1 pN·nm', k='statistical_mechanics soft_matter', w='place:cell')
n('bio_microbial_ecology_elective','Microbial ecology and biogeochemistry','concept','bio_microbial_metabolism bio_biogeochemical_cycles_concept bio_metagenomics','Microbes running the planet\'s chemistry.',
 'Microbes drive the carbon, nitrogen and sulfur cycles. In soil and oceans they decompose, fix nitrogen and produce or consume greenhouse gases.',
 'Nitrification, denitrification and anammox shape the nitrogen budget; methanogens and methanotrophs control methane.',
 'Marine Prochlorococcus (≈ 10²⁷ cells) is the most abundant photosynthesiser. Microbial loop and viral shunt shape ocean carbon export. Soil microbial carbon-use efficiency is a key uncertain parameter in climate models.',
 'Prochlorococcus cells|≈ 3×10²⁷;Marine microbes share of ocean respiration|≈ 70–90%', 'MIC:8.7 BIO:22.3', k='biogeochemical_cycles soil_biology', w='place:soil')
n('bio_bioethics_society','Bioethics and biology in society','concept','bio_research_ethics bio_gene_therapy','Questions technology raises.',
 'Biology raises ethical questions: gene editing of embryos, GMOs, privacy of genetic data, animal use and access to treatments.',
 'The 2018 birth of CRISPR-edited babies in China was widely condemned. Ethical frameworks weigh autonomy, beneficence, non-maleficence and justice.',
 'Policy responds through oversight bodies, international summits and law (GINA in the US). Equity of benefit, dual-use risks and public engagement are active governance areas, and views legitimately differ.',
 'CRISPR babies announced|2018 (He Jiankui);GINA enacted|2008', 'BET:1.1', k='medicine_evidence', w='')
save('bio_electives')

# ---------------- Processes ----------------
section('bio_processes', 'Processes (how life works)',
 'Step-by-step walkthroughs of the key processes of life.',
 'Many biological ideas are really stories with a sequence of steps. This section lines them up so you can watch them unfold, from copying DNA to building an embryo.',
 'Each process links to the molecules, organelles and cells where it happens.',
 'Processes are summaries of branching, regulated, noisy networks; steps shown are the canonical pathway.',
 'central_dogma')
use('cellular_respiration protein_synthesis mitosis action_potential synaptic_transmission muscle_contraction cardiac_cycle breathing digestion urine_formation blood_clotting immune_response hormone_signaling thermoregulation vision bone_remodeling')
proc('dna_replication_process','DNA replication','bio_replication_machinery','Copying the genome before cell division.',
 'The cell unzips its DNA and builds a new partner strand for each old strand, so two identical double helices result.',
 'Replication starts at origins, runs in both directions, and is checked for mistakes as it goes.',
 'Fork speed ≈ 1,000 nt/s (E. coli); in human HeLa cells a mean of 0.68 kb/min (≈ 11 nt/s), at most ≈ 2 kb/min; error rate ≈ 10⁻⁹–10⁻¹⁰ per base after proofreading and mismatch repair.',
 'Human origins|≈ 30,000–50,000;Okazaki fragment length|≈ 100–200 nt (eukaryotes)', 'BIO:14.3 BIO:14.4 BIO:14.5',
 [('Origin recognition','Initiator proteins bind an origin of replication and load the helicase.','nucleus bio_dna_structure'),
  ('Unwinding','Helicase separates the two strands, forming two replication forks; single-strand binding proteins and topoisomerase relieve strain.','bio_replication_machinery'),
  ('Priming','Primase lays down short RNA primers to give DNA polymerase a starting point.','bio_replication_machinery'),
  ('Leading strand','DNA polymerase extends the new strand continuously in the 5′→3′ direction towards the fork.','bio_nucleotides'),
  ('Lagging strand','The other strand is made in Okazaki fragments, each needing a primer.','bio_replication_machinery'),
  ('Joining and proofreading','Primers are replaced, ligase seals nicks and proofreading and mismatch repair correct errors.','bio_dna_damage_repair')],
 w='place:dna', k='dna_structure')
proc('transcription_process','Transcription','bio_transcription_eukaryotic bio_rna_processing','Copying a gene into RNA.',
 'RNA polymerase reads one strand of DNA and builds a matching RNA molecule.',
 'In eukaryotes the new RNA is capped, spliced and tailed before it leaves the nucleus.',
 'Pol II elongates ≈ 1–4 kb/min; the Pol II CTD coordinates RNA processing. Bacterial transcription and translation are coupled.',
 'Elongation rate (human Pol II)|≈ 1–4 kb/min;Promoter box|TATA at ≈ −30', 'BIO:15.2 BIO:15.3 BIO:15.4',
 [('Initiation','Transcription factors and RNA polymerase assemble at the promoter.','bio_eukaryotic_gene_regulation'),
  ('Promoter opening','The DNA is opened to a bubble of ≈ 12–14 bp and the template strand enters the polymerase.','bio_transcription_prokaryotic'),
  ('Elongation','The polymerase adds ribonucleotides complementary to the template, 5′→3′.','bio_rna_types'),
  ('Termination','A terminator signal causes the polymerase to release RNA and DNA.','bio_transcription_prokaryotic'),
  ('Capping and polyadenylation','A 5′ cap and a poly-A tail are added to pre-mRNA.','bio_rna_processing'),
  ('Splicing and export','The spliceosome removes introns; the mature mRNA exits through nuclear pores.','bio_nuclear_organization')],
 w='place:dna', k='central_dogma')
proc('glycolysis_process','Glycolysis (steps)','bio_glycolysis','Ten steps from glucose to pyruvate.',
 'One glucose is split in two halves with a small net profit of ATP and NADH.',
 'It has an investment phase (uses 2 ATP) and a payoff phase (makes 4 ATP and 2 NADH).',
 'PFK-1 is the main control point; steps with ΔG ≪ 0 are hexokinase, PFK-1 and pyruvate kinase.',
 'Net ATP|2;NADH|2', 'BIO:7.2 ORG:29.5',
 [('Phosphorylation','Hexokinase adds a phosphate to glucose, trapping it as glucose-6-phosphate (−1 ATP).','mol:glucose_6_phosphate'),
  ('Isomerisation and second phosphate','G6P becomes fructose-6-phosphate; PFK-1 adds a second phosphate (−1 ATP), the committed step.','bio_allostery'),
  ('Splitting','Aldolase cleaves the six-carbon sugar into two three-carbon sugars.','bio_carbonyl_chemistry'),
  ('Oxidation','G3P is oxidised and phosphorylated, making NADH.','mol:nadh'),
  ('Substrate-level phosphorylation','Phosphate groups are transferred to ADP, making 4 ATP in all.','mol:atp'),
  ('Pyruvate','Pyruvate kinase makes pyruvate, which goes to the mitochondrion or to fermentation.','mol:pyruvate')],
 w='mol:glucose_6_phosphate', k='metabolism_overview', mol='glucose_alpha pyruvate atp nadh')
proc('krebs_cycle_process','Citric acid cycle (steps)','bio_krebs_cycle','Eight steps that oxidise acetyl units.',
 'An acetyl group joins a four-carbon molecule, is oxidised around a loop, and leaves as CO₂.',
 'Each turn makes 3 NADH, 1 FADH₂ and 1 GTP.',
 'α-Ketoglutarate dehydrogenase and isocitrate dehydrogenase are key regulated steps.',
 'NADH per turn|3;CO₂ per turn|2', 'BIO:7.3 ORG:29.7',
 [('Condensation','Acetyl-CoA joins oxaloacetate to make citrate.','mol:citrate'),
  ('Isomerisation','Citrate is rearranged to isocitrate.','mol:citrate'),
  ('First decarboxylation','Isocitrate is oxidised to α-ketoglutarate, releasing CO₂ and NADH.','mol:alpha_ketoglutarate'),
  ('Second decarboxylation','α-Ketoglutarate is oxidised to succinyl-CoA, releasing CO₂ and NADH.','mol:alpha_ketoglutarate'),
  ('Substrate-level phosphorylation','Succinyl-CoA yields succinate and GTP.','mol:succinate'),
  ('Regeneration','Succinate, fumarate and malate are oxidised, giving FADH₂ and NADH, and regenerating oxaloacetate.','mol:oxaloacetate')],
 w='mol:citrate', k='metabolism_overview', mol='citrate oxaloacetate nadh fad')
proc('oxidative_phosphorylation_process','Oxidative phosphorylation','bio_chemiosmosis_atp_synthase bio_electron_transport_chain','From electrons to ATP.',
 'Electrons from NADH and FADH₂ flow to oxygen, pumping protons that power ATP synthase.',
 'The proton gradient across the inner mitochondrial membrane is the energy store.',
 '≈ 10 H⁺ pumped per NADH; ≈ 4 H⁺ per ATP including phosphate transport; P/O ≈ 2.5 for NADH.',
 'P/O ratio (NADH)|≈ 2.5;Total ATP per glucose|≈ 30–32', 'BIO:7.4 MIC:8.3',
 [('Electron donation','NADH gives electrons to Complex I; FADH₂ gives them to Complex II.','mol:nadh'),
  ('Proton pumping','Complexes I, III and IV pump protons from matrix to intermembrane space.','mitochondrion'),
  ('Carriers','Ubiquinone and cytochrome c ferry electrons between complexes.','mol:ubiquinone_q1'),
  ('Oxygen reduction','Complex IV passes electrons to O₂, making water.','mol:oxygen'),
  ('Gradient','The proton gradient (Δp ≈ 150–200 mV) accumulates.','bio_membrane_potential'),
  ('ATP synthesis','Protons flow through ATP synthase, driving ADP + Pi → ATP.','mol:atp')],
 w='mitochondrion', k='oxidative_phosphorylation', mol='nadh atp oxygen')
proc('photosynthesis_light_process','Photosynthesis: light reactions','bio_light_reactions','Light to ATP and NADPH.',
 'Light energy excites electrons in chlorophyll; the electrons make ATP and NADPH and water is split to oxygen.',
 'Two photosystems work in series in the thylakoid membrane.',
 'The Z-scheme combines PSII (P680), cytochrome b6f, PSI (P700) and ATP synthase; ≈ 8 photons per O₂.',
 'Photons per O₂ (minimum)|≈ 8;Products|ATP, NADPH, O₂', 'BIO:8.2',
 [('Light capture','Antenna pigments absorb photons and funnel energy to the PSII reaction centre.','mol:chlorophyll_a'),
  ('Water splitting','The oxygen-evolving complex splits water, releasing O₂, protons and electrons.','mol:water'),
  ('Electron transport','Electrons travel through plastoquinone and cytochrome b6f, pumping protons into the lumen.','bio_electron_transport_chain'),
  ('Second photosystem','PSI re-excites the electrons.','bio_light_reactions'),
  ('NADPH formation','Ferredoxin and NADP⁺ reductase make NADPH.','bio_redox_biology'),
  ('ATP formation','Protons exit via ATP synthase, forming ATP.','bio_chemiosmosis_atp_synthase')],
 w='mol:chlorophyll_a', k='photosynthesis_light', mol='chlorophyll_a water oxygen')
proc('calvin_cycle_process','Calvin cycle','bio_calvin_cycle','Fixing CO₂ into sugar.',
 'Plants attach CO₂ to a five-carbon sugar and reshuffle carbons to make sugar and regenerate the starter.',
 'Three phases: fixation, reduction and regeneration.',
 'Rubisco catalyses carboxylation of RuBP; the cycle consumes 3 ATP and 2 NADPH per CO₂.',
 'ATP per CO₂|3;NADPH per CO₂|2', 'BIO:8.3 COB:5.3',
 [('Fixation','Rubisco joins CO₂ to RuBP, making two 3-phosphoglycerate.','mol:carbon_dioxide'),
  ('Phosphorylation','ATP converts 3-PGA to 1,3-bisphosphoglycerate.','mol:atp'),
  ('Reduction','NADPH reduces it to glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate (G3P).','mol:glyceraldehyde'),
  ('Export','One G3P leaves for every three CO₂ fixed.','bio_polysaccharides'),
  ('Regeneration','The remaining G3P is rearranged to RuBP using ATP.','bio_calvin_cycle'),
  ('Control','Light activates the enzymes through pH, Mg²⁺ and thioredoxin.','bio_metabolic_regulation')],
 w='place:leaf', k='photosynthesis_light', mol='carbon_dioxide glyceraldehyde')
proc('meiosis_process','Meiosis','bio_meiosis_variation','Halving the chromosome number.',
 'One diploid cell divides twice to make four haploid cells with new gene combinations.',
 'Homologues pair and swap pieces in prophase I; sister chromatids separate in meiosis II.',
 'Chiasmata hold homologues together; cohesin protection at centromeres lets sisters stay joined until meiosis II.',
 'Daughter cells|4 haploid;Human crossovers|≈ 40–60 per meiosis', 'BIO:11.1 COB:7.2',
 [('Prophase I','Homologous chromosomes pair (synapsis) and crossing over occurs.','bio_recombination_transposons'),
  ('Metaphase I','Homologous pairs align at the equator; orientation is random.','cytoskeleton'),
  ('Anaphase I','Homologues are pulled to opposite poles; sisters stay together.','bio_microtubules_motors'),
  ('Telophase I','Two haploid cells form, each with duplicated chromosomes.','bio_cytokinesis_prokaryotic_division'),
  ('Meiosis II','Sister chromatids separate as in mitosis.','mitosis'),
  ('Gametes','Four genetically distinct haploid cells result.','sperm egg_cell')],
 w='place:cell', k='genetics_inheritance')
